@albanD There is inconsistency between python API and C++ API.

[Python](https://github.com/pytorch/pytorch/blob/ad22f0ffb456fc3f967ad32e09376f7c9cf94a56/torch/nn/functional.py#L896-L897):
```python
for d in range(len(kernel_size)):
    default_size.append((input_size[-len(kernel_size) + d] - 1) * stride[d] + kernel_size[d] - 2 * padding[d])
```
[C++](https://github.com/pytorch/pytorch/blob/1cc002621d23749c6742193ef3c1eda1015480c2/torch/csrc/api/include/torch/nn/functional/pooling.h#L638-L641):
```c++
for (const auto d : c10::irange(kernel_size.size())) {
  default_size.push_back(
      (input_size[d + 2] - 1) * stride[d] + kernel_size[d] - 2 * padding[d]);
}
```
In python frontend, the base index of `input_size` is `len(input_size) - len(kernel_size) - 1` while C++ frontend simply uses `2`. It seems clear than the length of `kernel_size`, `stride`, and `padding` should be same, but I'm not sure about `input_size`.

Segfault does not occur on `stride` and `padding` when `_unpool_output_size` is called from `max_unpool*d` API, because lengths of them are automatically set by `ExpandingArray`.

But still,
```c++
for (const auto d : c10::irange(kernel_size.size())) {
  default_size.push_back(
      (input_size[d + 2] - 1) * stride[d] + kernel_size[d] - 2 * padding[d]);
}
```
this C++ code should be fixed as it is not consistent with [document](https://pytorch.org/docs/master/generated/torch.nn.MaxUnpool1d.html?highlight=maxunpool1d#torch.nn.MaxUnpool1d). `input_size[d + 2]` seems like it only considers (N ,C ,*) shapes, while document(and python API) allows (C, *) shapes. I guess replacing `input_size[d + 2]` with `input_size[input_size.size() - kernel_size.size() - 1]` would work.

```c++
#include <stdint.h>
#include <stddef.h>
#include <c10/util/irange.h>
#include <cassert>
#include <torch/torch.h>

namespace F = torch::nn::functional;
using namespace torch::nn;

int main() {
  try {
    torch::TensorOptions toptions = torch::TensorOptions();

    auto foptions =
      F::MaxUnpool1dFuncOptions(torch::ExpandingArray<1>({1}))
        .stride({1})
        .padding({1});

    auto result =
      F::max_unpool1d(
        torch::randn({1}, toptions),
        torch::randn({1}, toptions),
        foptions);
  } catch (std::exception& e) {
    std::cout << "Exception: " << e.what();
  }

  return 0;
}
```
This code executes `input_size[d + 2]` silently where `input_size.size()` is `1` and `d + 2` is `2`. I don't know why ASAN does not catch it, but when I slightly modify [`pooling.h` code](https://github.com/pytorch/pytorch/blob/1cc002621d23749c6742193ef3c1eda1015480c2/torch/csrc/api/include/torch/nn/functional/pooling.h#L638-L641) like below,
```c++
for (const auto d : c10::irange(kernel_size.size())) {
  default_size.push_back(
      //(input_size[d + 2] - 1) * stride[d] + kernel_size[d] - 2 * padding[d]);
      (input_size.at(d + 2) - 1) * stride[d] + kernel_size[d] - 2 * padding[d]);
}
```
